<section xml:id="lesson-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Lesson 02</title>

<exercise xml:id="lesson-2-describe-functions" workspace="3in">
  <introduction>
        <p>For each of the following functions, describe the following:</p>
            <ul>
                  <li><p>Intervals on which the function is increasing. If none, write <em>none</em>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Intervals on which the function is decreasing. If none, write <em>none</em>.</p></li>
                              <li><p>Intervals on which the function is constant. If none, write <em>none</em>.</p></li>
                                    <li><p>The numbers at which the function has a relative maximum. What are the relative maxima? If none, write <em>none</em>.</p></li>
                                          <li><p>The numbers at which the function has a relative minimum. What are the relative minima? If none, write <em>none</em>.</p></li>
                                              </ul>
                                                </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-graph-1">
        <caption>Function Graph 1</caption>
                <image>
                    <description>
                        A coordinate plane displaying a piecewise linear graph
                        with solid dots marking specific vertices.
                        The graph consists of connected line segments starting
                        at a point in the second quadrant, zigzagging up and
                        down across the axes, and ending with a horizontal ray
                        pointing to the right in the first quadrant.
                        Solid dots are located at x-coordinates -8, -5, -2, 0,
                        2, 5, and 7.
                    </description>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-piecewise-inc-dec-const">
<diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
  <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
    <grid hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" stroke="lightgray" spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))"/>
    <axes hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)" vlabels="(-8,2,8)" clear-background="yes"/>
    
    <line p1="(-8,6)" p2="(-5,2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue"/>
    <line p1="(-5,2)" p2="(-2,8)" thickness="2" stroke="blue"/>
    <line p1="(-2,8)" p2="(0,2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue"/>
    <line p1="(0,2)" p2="(2,-9)" thickness="2" stroke="blue"/>
    <line p1="(2,-9)" p2="(5,-5)" thickness="2" stroke="blue"/>
    <line p1="(5,-5)" p2="(7,1)" thickness="2" stroke="blue"/>
    <line p1="(7,1)" p2="(10,1)" thickness="2" arrows="1" stroke="blue"/>

    <point p="(-8,6)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue"/>
    <point p="(-5,2)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue"/>
    <point p="(-2,8)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue"/>
    <point p="(0,2)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue"/>
    <point p="(2,-9)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue"/>
    <point p="(5,-5)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue"/>
    <point p="(7,1)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>

    </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Read the graph from left to right, identifying where the <m>y</m>-values rise, fall, or stay flat. Look for the highest and lowest points in specific regions.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Determine visually from the graph's behavior.</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>To find intervals of increase, decrease, and constant behavior, trace the graph horizontally from left to right and record the <m>x</m>-intervals where the <m>y</m>-values rise, fall, or remain horizontal. Relative maxima occur at the <m>x</m>-coordinates of "peaks," and relative minima occur at the <m>x</m>-coordinates of "valleys."</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-graph-2">
        <caption>Function Graph 2</caption>
        <image source="Images/bc(review).png" width="50%" />
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Read the graph from left to right, identifying where the <m>y</m>-values rise, fall, or stay flat. Look for the highest and lowest points in specific regions.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Determine visually from the graph's behavior.</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>To find intervals of increase, decrease, and constant behavior, trace the graph horizontally from left to right and record the <m>x</m>-intervals where the <m>y</m>-values rise, fall, or remain horizontal. Relative maxima occur at the <m>x</m>-coordinates of "peaks," and relative minima occur at the <m>x</m>-coordinates of "valleys."</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-graph-3">
        <caption>Function Graph 3</caption>
        <image source="Images/PWDeval(review).png" width="50%" />
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Read the graph from left to right, identifying where the <m>y</m>-values rise, fall, or stay flat. Look for the highest and lowest points in specific regions.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Determine visually from the graph's behavior.</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>To find intervals of increase, decrease, and constant behavior, trace the graph horizontally from left to right and record the <m>x</m>-intervals where the <m>y</m>-values rise, fall, or remain horizontal. Relative maxima occur at the <m>x</m>-coordinates of "peaks," and relative minima occur at the <m>x</m>-coordinates of "valleys."</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lesson-2-complete-symmetry" workspace="3in">
  <introduction>
    <p>Complete the graph so that it has the specified symmetry.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Symmetry with respect to the <m>x</m>-axis</p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-sym-1">
        <caption>Graph for x-axis symmetry</caption>
        <image source="Images/compsym(review).png" width="50%" />
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Reflect the existing parts of the graph vertically across the horizontal axis.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Graph reflected across the <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>For every point <m>(x, y)</m> on the given graph, plot the corresponding point <m>(x, -y)</m> to create an <m>x</m>-axis reflection.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Symmetry with respect to the <m>y</m>-axis</p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-sym-2">
        <caption>Graph for y-axis symmetry</caption>
        <image source="Images/compsym(review).png" width="50%" />
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Reflect the existing parts of the graph horizontally across the vertical axis.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Graph reflected across the <m>y</m>-axis.</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>For every point <m>(x, y)</m> on the given graph, plot the corresponding point <m>(-x, y)</m> to create a <m>y</m>-axis reflection.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Symmetry with respect to the origin</p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-sym-3">
        <caption>Graph for origin symmetry</caption>
        <image source="Images/compsym(review).png" width="50%" />
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Reflect the graph across both axes, or rotate it 180 degrees around the origin.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Graph reflected across the origin.</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>For every point <m>(x, y)</m> on the given graph, plot the corresponding point <m>(-x, -y)</m> to create origin symmetry.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Symmetric, but not with respect to the <m>x</m>-axis, <m>y</m>-axis, or origin</p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-2-sym-4">
        <caption>Graph for custom symmetry</caption>
        <image source="Images/compsym(review).png" width="50%" />
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Choose an arbitrary line (like <m>y = x</m>) or point other than the origin and reflect the graph across it.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Graph reflected across a custom line or point.</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>A common alternative symmetry is reflection across the line <m>y = x</m>, where every point <m>(x, y)</m> becomes <m>(y, x)</m>. Alternatively, reflect across a vertical or horizontal line other than the axes.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lesson-2-even-odd-neither" workspace="3in">
  <introduction>
    <p>Determine if each of the following is even, odd, or neither.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p><m>y=x^4+3x^2-1</m></p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Substitute <m>-x</m> for <m>x</m> and see if you get the original function (even) or its exact opposite (odd).</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Even</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>Let <m>f(x) = x^4 + 3x^2 - 1</m>. We evaluate <m>f(-x)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f(-x) \amp= (-x)^4 + 3(-x)^2 - 1</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= x^4 + 3x^2 - 1</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= f(x)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Because <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>, the function is even.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p><m>x=y^3</m></p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Solve for <m>y</m> to write it as a function of <m>x</m>, then test <m>f(-x)</m>.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Odd</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>First, solve the equation for <m>y</m> to express it as a function <m>f(x)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>x \amp= y^3</mrow>
          <mrow>y \amp= \sqrt[3]{x}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt[3]{x}</m>. We evaluate <m>f(-x)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f(-x) \amp= \sqrt[3]{-x}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= -\sqrt[3]{x}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= -f(x)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Because <m>f(-x) = -f(x)</m>, the function is odd.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p><m>x^2+y^3=36</m></p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Solve for <m>y</m> to write it as a function of <m>x</m>, then test <m>f(-x)</m>.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Even</p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>First, solve the equation for <m>y</m> to express it as a function <m>f(x)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>x^2 + y^3 \amp= 36</mrow>
          <mrow>y^3 \amp= 36 - x^2</mrow>
          <mrow>y \amp= \sqrt[3]{36 - x^2}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt[3]{36 - x^2}</m>. We evaluate <m>f(-x)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f(-x) \amp= \sqrt[3]{36 - (-x)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \sqrt[3]{36 - x^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= f(x)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Because <m>f(-x) = f(x)</m>, the function is even.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lesson-2-graph-piecewise" workspace="4in">
  <introduction>
    <p>Graph the following functions, then list the domain and range.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <me>
          f(x) = \begin{cases} 
          x+5 \amp \text{if } -10 \leq x \lt -2 \\
          x^2+2x+3 \amp \text{if } x \geq -2 
          \end{cases}
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Graph the linear piece between <m>x = -10</m> and <m>x = -2</m> (open circle at <m>x=-2</m>). Graph the parabolic piece for <m>x \geq -2</m> (closed circle at <m>x=-2</m>).</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Domain: <m>[-10, \infty)</m>, Range: <m>[-5, \infty)</m></p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>The domain is given by combining the intervals of the pieces: <m>[-10, -2) \cup [-2, \infty) = [-10, \infty)</m>.</p>
      <p>To find the range, evaluate the outputs of each piece:</p>
      <p>For <m>x+5</m> on <m>[-10, -2)</m>, the outputs range from <m>-10+5 = -5</m> up to, but not including, <m>-2+5 = 3</m>. This gives a range segment of <m>[-5, 3)</m>.</p>
      <p>For <m>x^2+2x+3</m> on <m>[ -2, \infty)</m>, find the vertex. The <m>x</m>-coordinate is <m>x = \frac{-b}{2a} = \frac{-2}{2(1)} = -1</m>. The <m>y</m>-coordinate is <m>(-1)^2 + 2(-1) + 3 = 2</m>. The parabola opens upward, so its minimum value is <m>2</m>. This gives a range segment of <m>[2, \infty)</m>.</p>
      <p>Combining the range segments gives <m>[-5, 3) \cup [2, \infty) = [-5, \infty)</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <me>
          f(x) = \begin{cases} 
          x^2-1 \amp \text{if } x \leq 0 \\
          2x-1 \amp \text{if } 0 \lt x \leq 5 \\
          3 \amp \text{if } x \gt 5 
          \end{cases}
        </me>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Graph the parabola for <m>x \leq 0</m>, the straight line for <m>x</m> between <m>0</m> and <m>5</m>, and the horizontal line for <m>x > 5</m>.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p>Domain: <m>(-\infty, \infty)</m>, Range: <m>[-1, \infty)</m></p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>The domain includes all real numbers because the combined intervals cover <m>(-\infty, 0] \cup (0, 5] \cup (5, \infty) = (-\infty, \infty)</m>.</p>
      <p>To find the range, evaluate the outputs of each piece:</p>
      <p>For <m>x^2-1</m> on <m>(-\infty, 0]</m>, the minimum occurs at the vertex <m>(0, -1)</m> and opens upward, giving a range of <m>[-1, \infty)</m>.</p>
      <p>For <m>2x-1</m> on <m>(0, 5]</m>, the line segment goes from <m>y = 2(0)-1 = -1</m> (exclusive) to <m>y = 2(5)-1 = 9</m> (inclusive), giving a range of <m>(-1, 9]</m>.</p>
      <p>For <m>3</m> on <m>(5, \infty)</m>, the output is just the constant value <m>3</m>.</p>
      <p>Combining all range outputs: <m>[-1, \infty) \cup (-1, 9] \cup \{3\} = [-1, \infty)</m>.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lesson-2-difference-quotient" workspace="3in">
  <introduction>
    <p>Evaluate the difference quotient,<m>\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}</m>,  for each of the functions.</p>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p><m>f(x)=2x^2-x</m></p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Substitute <m>(x+h)</m> into the function carefully, expand, and cancel terms.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p><m>4x + 2h - 1</m></p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>First, find the expression for <m>f(x+h)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f(x+h) \amp= 2(x+h)^2 - (x+h)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 2(x^2 + 2xh + h^2) - x - h</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 2x^2 + 4xh + 2h^2 - x - h</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Now apply the difference quotient formula:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{(2x^2 + 4xh + 2h^2 - x - h) - (2x^2 - x)}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{2x^2 + 4xh + 2h^2 - x - h - 2x^2 + x}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{4xh + 2h^2 - h}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{h(4x + 2h - 1)}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 4x + 2h - 1</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p><m>f(x)=x^3-4</m></p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>Remember that <m>(x+h)^3 = x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3</m>.</p>
    </hint>
    <answer>
      <p><m>3x^2 + 3xh + h^2</m></p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
      <p>First, find the expression for <m>f(x+h)</m>:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>f(x+h) \amp= (x+h)^3 - 4</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= (x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3) - 4</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Now apply the difference quotient formula:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h} \amp= \frac{(x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3 - 4) - (x^3 - 4)}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{x^3 + 3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3 - 4 - x^3 + 4}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{3x^2h + 3xh^2 + h^3}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{h(3x^2 + 3xh + h^2)}{h}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= 3x^2 + 3xh + h^2</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</exercise>
    
</section>